The TLS Handshake(TLS 1.3)


Client                          Server
|                                  |
|-------------ClientHello--------->|
|                                  |
| TLS version                      |
| Supported cipher suites          |
| Key share(e.g. X25519)           |
| Random nonce                     |
|                                  |
|<-----------ServerHello-----------|
|                                  |
| Chosen cipher suite              |
| Key share(server's half)         |
| Random nonce                     |
|                                  |
| [Both sides derive session keys] |
|                                  |
|<---{Certificate}-----------------|
|<---{CertificateVerify}-----------|
|<---{Finished}--------------------|
|                                  |
|----{Finished}------------------->|
|                                  |
|<==Application Data (encrypted)==>|

{} = already encrypted wiht handshake keys TLS 1.3 achieves 1-RTT and supports 0-RTT
resumption for repeat connections.

Cipher Suites:

Component

Key Exchange   | Establish shared secret | X25519, P-256(ECDHE)           | RSA, DHE, ECDHE
Authentication | Verify server identity  | RSA-PSS, ECDSA                 | RSA, DSA
AEAD Cipher    | Encrypt + Integrity     | AES-256-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305 | AES-CBC(+HMAC separately)
Hash(PRF/HKDF) | Key derivation          | SHA-384, SHA-256               | SHA-256, MD5

TLS 1.3 cipher suites:

TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384
TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256
TLS_AES_128_CCM_SHA256
TLS_AES_128_CCM_8_SHA256

X.509 Certificates
A certificate is a signed data structure binding a public key to an identity.

Certificate
├── Version (v3)
├── Serial Number
├── Signature Algorithm (e.g. sha256WithRSAEncryption)
├── Issuer (CA's Distinguished Name)
├── Validity (notBefore / notAfter)
├── Subject (CN=example.com, O=Acme Corp, ...)
├── Subject Public Key Info
│   ├── Algorithm (RSA 2048, EC P-256, ...)
│   └── Public Key
└── Extensions (v3)
    ├── Subject Alternative Names (SANs)  ← actual hostnames checked
    ├── Key Usage / Extended Key Usage
    ├── Basic Constraints (isCA: true/false)
    ├── CRL Distribution Points
    └── Authority Information Access (OCSP URL)

PKI(Public Key Infrastructure)
PKI is the trust system that makes certificates meaningful.

Root CA  (self-signed, offline, in OS/browser trust store)
    │
    └── Intermediate CA  (online, issues end-entity certs)
            │
            ├── example.com  (leaf cert, presented by server)
            └── api.example.com
